<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhagavata Purana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Srimad Bhagavatam, Srimad Bhagavata Mahapurana, Bhagavata</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bhagavata Purana, also known as the Srimad Bhagavatam, is one of the eighteen major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> (Mahapuranas) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> and a central text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a>. Composed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and traditionally attributed to Veda <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyasa">Vyasa</a>, it promotes bhakti, devotion, towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, and treats cosmology, genealogy, legend and yoga. Its twelve books hold 335 chapters and 18,000 verses; the tenth, with about 4,000 verses, has been the most popular and widely studied. Scholars date it variously, many placing much of it in the Gupta period and others its final redaction in the 9th or early 10th century. It was the first Purana translated into a European language, in a French version of a Tamil text in 1788.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana.jpg" alt="Bhagavata Purana" width="330" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rajasthan_manuscript_page_of_Bhagavata_Purana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Bhagavata means follower or worshipper of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>, and Purana means ancient or old traditional history. The text calls Krishna Bhagavan, the Blessed One. Many Indologists give no precise date for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a>, because their material was passed on orally, and a date applies only to the text as it now stands. Sheridan, in a study of 1986, dated its composition between 500 and 1000 CE and argued that a version must have existed by 1030, when al-Biruni mentions it. Many scholars, Hardy among them, date its final redaction to the 9th or early 10th century. The oldest surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> dates to c. 1124-25 and is held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The twelve books consist of interwoven dialogues. The main reciter is Suka, who speaks to King Pariksit and quotes an earlier speaker, Narada, addressing King Yudhisthira; the text says that the first speaker was its composer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyasa">Vyasa</a>, whose son Suka is. The first book opens at the onset of the Kali Yuga with sages performing a thousand-year sacrifice in the forest of Naimisaranya. Later books cover creation, genealogies, avatars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> and Parashurama, and the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadu dynasty</a>. The tenth book, of 90 chapters, tells of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s life.</p><p>Its theology uses <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vedanta">Vedanta</a> and Samkhya in the service of bhakti. Traditions of Vedanta, among them the Advaita of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adi-shankara">Adi Shankara</a>, the qualified monism of Rāmānujachārya, the dualism of Madhavacharya and the Bhedābheda of Vallabha and Caitanya, have each read its verses through their own commentaries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Bhagavata Purana is widely recognised as the best known and most influential of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> and is called the Fifth Veda. It is among the most commented texts in Indian literature: over eighty medieval commentaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> alone are known, and a version exists in almost every Indian language. Its legends are retold in festivals such as Holi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwali">Diwali</a>, it inspired Ras Leela, dramatic enactments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s pastimes, and it played a significant part in the Krishna-bhakti movement of Chaitanya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><p>A French translation of a Tamil version appeared in 1788, and the Bhagavata was the first Purana translated into a European language. In the 20th century it spread beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and was translated into over twenty languages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhagavata Purana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Bhagavata Purana</b>, whose final form many scholars date to the 9th or early 10th century and trace to the south, carried this devotion to Krishna into Sanskrit and became its great scripture.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Chaitanya's followers used the Gita and the <b>Bhagavata Purana</b> as the school's primary scriptural sources.</blockquote><a href="/work/bhagavad-gita">Bhagavad Gita · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>Narrative reliefs along the balustrades tell the Ramayana, from the Shiva temple into the Brahma temple, and the stories of Krishna from the <b>Bhagavata Purana</b> on the Vishnu temple.</blockquote><a href="/work/prambanan">Prambanan · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
